    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Nausea and vomiting of pregnancy, moderate and worsening (O21.9)</content>. PUQE-24 score 11 with 4.3 percent weight loss. Sodium 136, potassium 3.6, chloride 99, bicarbonate 26 mmol/L, urea nitrogen 11, creatinine 0.58, and glucose 74 mg/dL are normal, with potassium at the low end. Urine ketones 40 mg/dL are recorded but do not measure dehydration. She still keeps fluids down, so she does not yet meet the definition of hyperemesis gravidarum, but she is close to it. [S]<list><item>Raise doxylamine-pyridoxine to four tablets a day: one in the morning, one mid-afternoon, and two at bedtime.</item><item>Add metoclopramide 10 mg three times a day before meals, as Dr. Oyelaran-Hughes advised; stop it and call if restlessness or abnormal movements develop.</item><item>Ondansetron discussed, including the very small absolute increase in orofacial clefting with first-trimester use; she prefers to keep it in reserve and will accept it if these steps fail.</item><item>Same-day review if she cannot keep fluids down for 24 hours, feels faint, passes little urine, or loses more than 5 percent of her pre-pregnancy weight (below 43.7 kg).</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">First pregnancy at 38 (O09.519)</content>. Intake results: blood group O, RhD positive, antibody screen negative; rubella immune; hepatitis B surface antigen, HIV, syphilis, and hepatitis C tests negative; urine culture no growth; interferon-gamma release assay negative, so there is no latent tuberculosis infection and no chest radiograph is needed. Hemoglobin 12.8 g/dL. She would like cell-free DNA screening from 10 weeks and a nuchal translucency scan at 11 to 14 weeks, and does not want diagnostic testing unless a screen is positive. [S]<list><item>Cell-free DNA screening at the next visit.</item><item>Nuchal translucency scan at 12 to 13 weeks.</item><item>Low-dose aspirin assessment at 12 weeks: two moderate risk factors.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PUQE-24 score (recorded in the note; no LOINC code found)</td><td>Every visit while nausea and vomiting last</td><td>Completed today</td><td>11 (moderate), up from 10</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight against pre-pregnancy weight (46.0 kg), pulse, blood pressure, and hydration</td><td>Every visit while nausea and vomiting last</td><td>Completed today</td><td>44.0 kg, down 4.3 percent; pulse 92; no orthostatic drop; mucous membranes slightly dry</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Electrolytes, urea nitrogen, creatinine, and glucose</td><td>When vomiting is moderate or severe, daily while on intravenous fluids, and until stable on oral intake</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Normal; potassium 3.6 mmol/L at the low end</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Urine ketones</td><td>With vomiting; recorded but not a measure of severity or dehydration</td><td>Completed today</td><td>40 mg/dL</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>TSH and free T4</td><td>In hyperemesis gravidarum; repeat at 16 to 18 weeks if suppressed</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not yet needed: no hyperemesis; to be checked if it develops</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antiemetic effects: drowsiness with doxylamine-pyridoxine; restlessness and abnormal movements with metoclopramide</td><td>Every visit and after each change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No daytime drowsiness; metoclopramide starts today with counseling to stop it if restlessness or abnormal movements occur</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>First prenatal tests (blood group, antibody screen, rubella, hepatitis B, HIV, syphilis, hepatitis C, urine culture)</td><td>At the first prenatal visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Reviewed today: all normal or negative</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Interferon-gamma release assay for tuberculosis</td><td>Once, for household tuberculosis exposure</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Negative; reviewed with her</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Maternal-fetal medicine consultation</td><td>Early in a first pregnancy at 38 with worsening nausea and vomiting</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen today; escalation thresholds, screening, and aspirin plan agreed</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Aneuploidy screening choice</td><td>Offered before 10 weeks</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Chose cell-free DNA from 10 weeks with a nuchal translucency scan; declined diagnostic testing unless a screen is positive</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure</td><td>Every prenatal visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>102/64 mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Fetal heart</td><td>Every prenatal visit once audible by handheld Doppler (from about 10 to 12 weeks)</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not yet reliably audible by Doppler at 8 weeks; cardiac activity was seen on the dating scan a week ago</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Nausea and vomiting mild (PUQE-24 score 6 or less) and weight back to her pre-pregnancy 46.0 kg by about 17 weeks. In progress; target 2026-03-24 [S]</item><item>Hydration and electrolytes normal within two weeks of any admission. In progress; target 2026-02-10 [S]</item><item>An informed choice about aneuploidy screening and diagnostic testing by 13 weeks. In progress; target 2026-02-24 [S]</item><item>Low-dose aspirin started between 12 and 16 weeks if indicated. In progress; target 2026-02-24 [S]</item><item>Tuberculosis screening completed and explained by the second visit. Outcome by 2026-01-20: Interferon-gamma release assay negative; no latent infection; no chest radiograph needed [S]</item><item>Mood screened in each trimester, with support offered. In progress; target 2026-03-24 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: ACOG Practice Bulletin No. 189: Nausea and Vomiting of Pregnancy (2018); The Management of Nausea and Vomiting in Pregnancy and Hyperemesis Gravidarum (Green-top Guideline No. 69) (2024); Screening for Fetal Chromosomal Abnormalities: ACOG Practice Bulletin, Number 226 (2020); Latent Tuberculosis Infection in Adults: Screening (2023)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Review in about three weeks, at 11 weeks, sooner by telephone or same day if the thresholds are reached. Next scheduled visit 2026-02-10: prenatal visit at 11 weeks: nausea and vomiting review and cell-free DNA screening. [S]</paragraph></text>

